<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Elster</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Weisse Elster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White Elster is a river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 250 kilometres long, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> near Aš and flows through the Vogtland past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greiz">Greiz</a> and Gera to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, joining the Saale near Halle. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its upper valley was ruled by the advocates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weida">Weida</a>, ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-elster.jpg" alt="White Elster" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans-Peter from Gera, Germany · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elstertal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
